.TH SED 1 "September 11, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
sed \- stream editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sed
[
.B \-n
] script [ file ] ...
.br
.B sed
[
.B \-n
] [
.B \-e
script ] ... [
.B \-f
sfile ] ... [ file ] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sed
copies the named
.I files
(standard input default) to the standard output,
edited according to a script of commands.
Each
.B \-e
adds its argument to the script and each
.B \-f
adds the contents of
.IR sfile ;
the pieces are joined with newlines, in order.
With neither, the first operand is the script.
The
.B \-n
option suppresses the default output, as does a first
script line that is exactly `#n'.
.PP
A script consists of editing commands, one per line
or separated by `;', of the form
.IP
[address [, address] ] [!] function [arguments]
.PP
Blanks may precede the function, and a `#' begins a comment
that runs to the end of the line.
.PP
In normal operation
.I sed
cyclically copies a line of input into a
.I pattern space
(unless there is something left after a `D' command),
applies in sequence all commands whose
.I addresses
select that pattern space,
and at the end of the script copies the pattern space
to the standard output (except under
.BR \-n )
and deletes it.
A second buffer, the
.IR "hold space" ,
is empty at the start and is touched only by the
`g', `G', `h', `H' and `x' commands.
Each space holds up to 2047 characters; a space grown past that
by `s', `N', `G' or `H' is a fatal error, not a truncation.
An input line longer than 255 characters is truncated there.
.PP
An
.I address
is either a decimal number that counts input lines cumulatively
across files, a `$' that addresses the last line of input, or a
context address `/regular expression/' \- or `\ecREc' with any
other single character
.I c
as the delimiter \- in the style of
.IR ed (1),
where the escape `\en' matches the newline embedded in the
pattern space by `N' or `G' and an empty regular expression
`//' means the last one used.
.PP
A command with no addresses selects every pattern space.
A command with one address selects each pattern space that
matches the address.
A command with two addresses selects the inclusive range from
the first pattern space that matches the first address through
the next pattern space that matches the second.
(If the second address is a number less than or equal to the
line number first selected, only one line is selected.)
Thereafter the process is repeated, looking again for the first
address.
A `!' after the addresses applies the function to the pattern
spaces
.I not
selected.
.PP
In the following list the maximum number of addresses each
function takes is in parentheses.
An argument denoted
.I text
consists of one or more lines, all but the last of which end
with `\e' to hide the newline; any other `\e' in text is removed
and the next character taken literally.
The V7 form `a\e' followed by a newline and the text is the
portable one; `a text' on one line is also accepted
(leading blanks dropped; `a\etext' keeps them).
An argument denoted
.I rfile
or
.I wfile
must terminate the command line.
Each
.I wfile
is created before processing begins;
`/dev/stdout' names the standard output.
There can be at most 4 distinct
.I wfile
arguments, and at most 48 commands and 16 labels in a script.
.TP
(1)\|a\e
.br
.ns
.TP
.I text
Append.
Place
.I text
on the output before reading the next input line.
.TP
(2)\|b\ label
Branch to the `:' command bearing the
.IR label .
If
.I label
is empty, branch to the end of the script.
A label ends at the end of the line or at a `;'.
.TP
(2)\|c\e
.br
.ns
.TP
.I text
Change.
Delete the pattern space.
With 0 or 1 address or at the end of a 2-address range, place
.I text
on the output.
Start the next cycle.
.TP
(2)\|d
Delete the pattern space.
Start the next cycle.
.TP
(2)\|D
Delete the initial segment of the pattern space through the
first newline.
Start the next cycle without reading new input.
.TP
(2)\|g
Replace the contents of the pattern space by the contents of
the hold space.
.TP
(2)\|G
Append the contents of the hold space to the pattern space.
.TP
(2)\|h
Replace the contents of the hold space by the contents of the
pattern space.
.TP
(2)\|H
Append the contents of the pattern space to the hold space.
.TP
(1)\|i\e
.br
.ns
.TP
.I text
Insert.
Place
.I text
on the standard output.
.TP
(2)\|l
List the pattern space on the standard output in an
unambiguous form:
`\e\e', `\ea', `\eb', `\ef', `\en', `\er', `\et' and `\ev' as those
escapes, other non-printing characters as three-digit octal
`\e\fIddd\fR', a `$' at the end, and long lines folded at 69
characters with a `\e'.
.TP
(2)\|n
Copy the pattern space to the standard output (unless under
.BR \-n ).
Replace the pattern space with the next line of input.
If there is no next line, quit without running the rest of the
script.
.TP
(2)\|N
Append the next line of input to the pattern space with an
embedded newline.
(The current line number changes.)
If there is no next line, quit
.I without
copying the pattern space to the output; `$!N' is the portable
spelling.
.TP
(2)\|p
Print.
Copy the pattern space to the standard output.
.TP
(2)\|P
Copy the initial segment of the pattern space through the first
newline to the standard output.
.TP
(1)\|q\ [exit-code]
Quit.
Branch to the end of the script.
Do not start a new cycle.
The exit status is
.IR exit-code ,
or 0.
.TP
(2)\|r\ rfile
Read the contents of
.IR rfile .
Place them on the output before reading the next input line.
A file that cannot be read is silently nothing.
.TP
(2)\|s/regular expression/replacement/flags
Substitute the
.I replacement
string for instances of the
.I regular expression
in the pattern space.
Any character may be used instead of `/'.
For a fuller description see
.IR ed (1)
and REGULAR EXPRESSIONS below.
.I Flags
is zero or more of
.RS
.TP
g
Global.
Substitute for all nonoverlapping instances of the
.I regular expression
rather than just the first one.
.TP
p
Print the pattern space if a replacement was made.
.TP
.I n
.I n
= 1 \- 512.
Substitute for just the
.IR n th
occurrence of the
.IR "regular expression" ;
with
.B g
as well, the
.IR n th
and every later one.
.TP
w\ wfile
Write.
Append the pattern space to
.I wfile
if a replacement was made.
.RE
.IP
In the
.I replacement
an `&' stands for the matched text, `\e1' to `\e9' for the text
matched by the corresponding `\e(' `\e)' subexpression, `\en' for a
newline, and a `\e' before any other character (a newline
included) for that character itself.
.TP
(2)\|t\ label
Test.
Branch to the `:' command bearing the
.I label
if any substitutions have been made since the most recent
reading of an input line or execution of a `t'.
If
.I label
is empty, branch to the end of the script.
.TP
(2)\|w\ wfile
Write.
Append the pattern space to
.IR wfile .
.TP
(2)\|x
Exchange the contents of the pattern and hold spaces.
.TP
(2)\|y/string1/string2/
Transform.
Replace all occurrences of characters in
.I string1
with the corresponding character in
.IR string2 .
The lengths of
.I string1
and
.I string2
must be equal.
`\en' is a newline, `\e\e' a backslash.
.TP
(2)\|!\ function
Don't.
Apply the
.I function
(or group, if
.I function
is `{') only to lines
.I not
selected by the address(es).
.TP
(0)\|:\ label
This command does nothing; it bears a
.I label
for `b' and `t' commands to branch to.
.TP
(1)\|=
Place the current line number on the standard output as a line.
.TP
(2)\|{
Execute the following commands through a matching `}' only
when the pattern space is selected.
.TP
(0)\|
An empty command is ignored.
.SH "REGULAR EXPRESSIONS"
The regular expressions are POSIX basic regular expressions
(the one engine shared with
.IR ed (1),
.IR ex (1),
.IR vi (1)
and
.IR grep (1)):
an ordinary character matches itself;
`.' matches any character;
`[\fIset\fR]' matches any character of the set, `[^\fIset\fR]' any
character not in it, with ranges `a\-z' and the classes
[:alpha:] [:digit:] [:alnum:] [:upper:] [:lower:] [:space:]
[:blank:] [:punct:] [:print:] [:cntrl:] [:graph:] [:xdigit:];
`\e(' `\e)' group, and `\e1' to `\e9' match what the corresponding
group matched;
`*' matches zero or more of the preceding item, `\e{\fIn\fR,\fIm\fR\e}'
between
.I n
and
.I m
of it (`\e{\fIn\fR\e}' exactly
.IR n ,
`\e{\fIn\fR,\e}' at least
.IR n ),
and `\e+' and `\e?' one or more and zero or one;
`^' at the start and `$' at the end anchor the match, `\en'
matches the embedded newline, and `\e' before any other
character takes it literally.
A bare `{', `+', `?', `(' or `)' is an ordinary character.
The match is the leftmost one, and the longest at that position.
There is no alternation and no `\-E'.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/SED  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ed(1), ex(1), grep(1), more(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A script error is reported as
.RI "`sed: " "message" " near char " N "'",
.I N
being the offset into the assembled script text; the exit status
is 2.
Exit status is otherwise 0, the argument of `q', or 2 when a
file could not be opened (the remaining input is still
processed) or a space overflowed.
.SH BUGS
An input line is cut at 255 characters.
`N' at the end of input follows POSIX and BSD (no output), not
GNU.
A `\e' before the delimiter inside a bracket expression is not
special; the delimiter itself is literal there.
